{
  "set": "C",
  "version": "2",
  "title": "Real-world spellings of set A dishes: Hinglish, typos, regional names and multi-item meals",
  "rules": [
    "Phrases are inputs only. Each one names the set A dish it means (`dish`); its reference is that dish's set A reference, so no new reference value is introduced.",
    "Production food_requests were not read for this version: the local database holds only 3 test phrases (dabeli, khandvi, kulcha) and none has a set A reference. A later version can add production phrases with the owner's approval.",
    "Two separate scores: matching (does rules-based logging match a food of the same dish without any AI?) and density (the AI estimate for the phrase's food words against the dish reference, with the dish hidden from the catalog).",
    "Multi-item phrases (`items`) are scored for matching only, one expected dish per item.",
    "v2: `expect` (a pattern for the logged food's name) makes a phrase's correct answer exact where its dish's name pattern is too broad (\"murgh korma\" must be chicken korma, not any korma). `dish: null` phrases are scored for matching only. `expect: null` means the catalog has no correct food: the phrase must not be logged (a suggestion is fine)."
